  "image_name": "4d3f82fb8770aa48c4e578698f376bf4.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several types of ferroic order and how they relate to domains, switching, and polarization. At the top, four idealized materials—ferroelastic, ferroelectric, ferromagnetic, and ferrotoroidic—are shown as neighboring domains with opposite internal states. Beneath each, a similar hysteresis loop indicates that strain, polarization, magnetization, or toroidization can be reversibly switched by an external field, highlighting the shared ferroic behavior of bistability and memory.\n\nThe middle section compares three routes to ferroelectricity. In “proper” displacive ferroelectrics, illustrated with BaTiO3, polarization comes directly from relative ionic shifts in the crystal. In “improper” ferroelectrics such as YMnO3, the polarization appears to be induced by a structural distortion rather than acting as the primary order parameter. In magnetically driven ferroelectricity, represented by TbMnO3, a noncollinear spin arrangement seems to generate electric polarization through spin coupling.\n\nThe bottom row focuses on ferroelectric domain walls. A side-by-side arrangement is neutral, while tail-to-tail and head-to-head configurations create negatively and positively charged walls, respectively. Overall, the figure emphasizes how different ferroic orders share common domain physics, while ferroelectricity can arise from distinct structural or magnetic mechanisms.",
